# ==== WRAPPED HEADERS · the `Block::header_start` discriminators (AXIS H)
#
# ⚠ INPUT→OUTPUT, not a fixpoint — see the `.expected` companion. `gg fmt`
# REJOINS a wrapped header onto one line, so the wrapped spelling exists only
# on pass one; the companion is a fixpoint and the harness's second pass
# asserts that.
#
# WHY THIS AXIS EXISTS. The orphan-pre-close flush decides membership by
# COLUMN: a comment indented past the block's HEADER line was written inside
# the block. Anchoring that test on the block's own `span.start` — the COLON —
# looks right and is wrong: on a wrapped header the colon sits on a
# CONTINUATION line whose indent is at or past the body's, so the test refuses
# everything and the tail comment escapes exactly as before, while its
# single-line sibling is fixed. Hence the typed `Block::header_start` field,
# written at the one indented-suite parser, pointing at the owning construct's
# FIRST line.
#
# ONE CELL PER VALUE-NEEDING ROW of that field's wiring table:
#   row 5 (the multi-kind block router)      — wrapped `while` condition, and
#                                              the clause-position `meta elif`
#   row 6 (the statement if/elif/else parser)— wrapped `if` and `elif`
#   row 7 (function bodies)                  — wrapped fn signature
#   row 8 (meta-match arm bodies)            — wrapped `meta match` case arm
#   row 1 (catch/rethrow bodies)             — wrapped `catch` header
#   row 9 (`meta while`)                     — wrapped `meta while` condition
#   the bracketed CONTAINER header           — enum/trait/equip cells live in
#                                              `header_colon_anchor_bracketed.gg`
#
# ⚠ ROW 9 NEEDS ITS OWN WRAPPED CELL, and for a while it did not have one. The
# `meta while` parser used to take its block span AFTER consuming the colon, so
# `Block.span.start` landed on the BODY's own line and even the UNWRAPPED cell
# in `orphan_close_routed_kinds.gg` discriminated. Hoisting that span onto the
# colon (parser hygiene, output-neutral) removed the accident: an unwrapped
# `meta while cond:` has its colon on the `meta` line, so a colon-seeded anchor
# now has the same indent as row 9's value and NO unwrapped cell can go red.
# Wrapping is what separates them again.
#
# ⚠ Two authoring traps this file works around: a wrapped bare `if` condition
# is a 10-error parse failure, so the PARENTHESIZED form is used; and the
# wrapped-FN cell only discriminates in its ALIGNED-CONTINUATION spelling — a
# hanging `):` at column 0 lets any anchor pass.
#
# RED, observed by BREAK-AND-WATCH on this tree (each break reverted after):
#   * value `header_start` from the block's own `span.start` (the colon):
#       wrapped-while tail  8 -> 4     wrapped-if tail       8 -> 4
#       wrapped-elif tail   8 -> 4     wrapped-fn tail       4 -> 0
#       wrapped meta-elif   8 -> 4     wrapped meta-while    8 -> 4
#       wrapped meta-match case tail  12 -> 8
#     Every one of those cells is in THIS file; the unwrapped siblings in
#     `orphan_close_routed_kinds.gg` stay green under this break, which is why
#     they are routed-kind cells and not discriminators.
#   * ROW 9: unwire meta-while's `header_start` (pass the colon-derived block
#       span instead of the `meta` keyword) — wrapped meta-while tail 8 -> 4,
#       and nothing else moves
#   * ROW 1: seed the catch/rethrow body from the colon instead of the LHS's
#       first line —              wrapped-catch tail  8 -> 4
#   * ROW 6: capture the `elif` clause span AFTER parsing the condition —
#       wrapped-elif tail  8 -> 4, while the wrapped-IF tail stays at 8
#   * ROW 8: seed the meta-match arm from its own colon instead of the `case`
#       keyword —                 wrapped meta-match case tail  12 -> 8

void wrapped_while(int i):
    while i < 3:
        print(i)
        # tail in a wrapped-while body

void wrapped_if(int i):
    if i < 3:
        print(i)
        # tail in a wrapped-if body
    elif i > 9:
        print(i)
        # tail in a wrapped-elif body

int wrapped_signature(int a_long_parameter_name, int another_long_parameter_name):
    print(a_long_parameter_name)
    return another_long_parameter_name
    # tail in a wrapped-signature body

void wrapped_meta_while[T]():
    meta while bitwidth(T) > 128:
        print("impossible")
        # tail in a wrapped meta-while body

void wrapped_meta_clauses[T]():
    meta if bitwidth(T) > 4096:
        print(1)
    elif bitwidth(T) > 2048:
        print(2)
        # tail in a wrapped meta-elif body

void wrapped_meta_match[T]():
    meta match typename(T):
        case "i" + "nt":
            print(1)
            # tail in a wrapped meta-match case body

int fallible(int x) throws String:
    if x < 0:
        throw "negative"
    return x

int wrapped_catch(int x):
    int a = fallible(x) catch (e):
        print("recovered")
        0
        # tail in a wrapped-catch body
    return a

void main():
    wrapped_while(1)
    wrapped_meta_while[int]()
    wrapped_if(1)
    print(wrapped_signature(1, 2))
    wrapped_meta_clauses[int]()
    wrapped_meta_match[int]()
    print(wrapped_catch(1))
